Well, let's take these questions one at a time.

First, the First Amendment issue.  I don't know what the legal
consensus actually is.  I do know, though, that UC Berkeley doesn't
get every group; especially, it doesn't get every alt group.  So, as a
practical matter, the news administrators here don't seem to think
that there are any serious First Amendment issues at stake; they seem
to think that if there's some good reason not to carry a group,
they're on reasonably safe ground if they drop it.  No matter what the
law says, I suspect that in practice they're right.  An additional
matter: I can believe that the First Amendment might be relevant if
the University of California chose which groups to carry, but I can't
believe that there would be a problem if they just chose to drop
Usenet altogether.  That's what I'm really suggesting might happen:
not that they'd drop one group, but that they'd decide that Usenet
as a whole carried too much risk of legal exposure.

Second, the possiblity of a backlash due to curtailing Usenet.
Frankly, I doubt if this is a serious danger.  I doubt if ending
Usenet would lead to any bad image in the press at all: it's not as if
the mass media think that Usenet is so wonderful in the first place.
And student protests?  If there were going to be student protests
about anything, they would have been over 150% tuition increases and
the abolition of whole departments; so far, there haven't been any
protests worth mentioning.  The risk of student protests nowadays is
overrated, particularly when the issue of concern, Usenet, just isn't
all that central to very many people's lives.

What I'm really trying to say it that very few people, and especially
very few people in power, really think that Usenet matters.  We on the
Net might think that Usenet is important, but we're atypical and our
priorities are distorted.  The position of Usenet in most major
organizations is quite marginal: it often receives no official
institutional support, and exists only because some sysadmin feels
like supporting it and because the institution as a whole doesn't care
enough, one way or another, to bother shutting it down.

If Usenet ever does present a serious threat to the large sites that
carry it---whether that's a threat of seriously bad publicity or of
adverse legal decisions---I think you'll see it ended.  I don't think
that the University of California Regents care enough about Usenet
to get into a serious fight for its preservation.
