Subject: Re: All alt groups banned
Date: 22 Jan 1995 18:58:01 GMT
Message-ID: <3fu9rp$bmh@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>

In article <3fu48s$2qr@eff.org>, Carl M. Kadie <kadie@eff.org> wrote:
>pboykin@math.gatech.edu (P. Oscar Boykin) writes:
>
>[...]
>>I am in the process of trying to get enough students angry about the fact 
>>the Georgia Tech, does not now, nor have they ever allowed any alt groups, 
>>not just groups like alt.sex.
>[...]
>
>To me this is like the library banning all magazines that start with the
>letter "P" because they don't want to subscribe to _Playboy_.
>

	There is another possibility, and while I don't have
information on this case, it is quite possible.  Perhaps, they don't
have enough space to store (or don't see the justification in
allocating space to store) all of Usenet.  So, they looked for a large
section of Usenet to strip.  Unlike your magazine example, the prefix
`alt' has meaning associated with it: it indicates that alt groups
have not gone through the formal process of news group creation that
other groups have undergone.  It's like a library deciding that they
will only subscribe to magazines, not to random pamphlets people want
to put on their shelves--or only subscribe to magazines from reputable
publishers.

	In short, this may not be content-based censorship, and while
I would certainly be annoyed if my university didn't carry the alt
groups that I read, I could understand if they had valid space
constraints.  Of course, if this were the case, I would work to set up
an alternative student-run news server that did carry the groups we
wanted to read.

--Sam

