In article <3fu48s$2qr@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

   pboykin@math.gatech.edu (P. Oscar Boykin) writes:

   [...]
   >I am in the process of trying to get enough students angry about the fact 
   >the Georgia Tech, does not now, nor have they ever allowed any alt groups, 
   >not just groups like alt.sex.
   [...]

   To me this is like the library banning all magazines that start with the
   letter "P" because they don't want to subscribe to _Playboy_.

No, it's like refusing papers they didn't subscribe to. It could be
because they don't have space for them, it could be for administrative
reasons (not having the time to administer the use of another couple
of thousand groups). I won't assume that it's an attempt to censor
conversation unless it were *specifically* against some group, rather
than a general "we won't carry that medium".

